WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.743
SPEAKER 1: Kubernetes--
I hear everyone

00:00:01.743 --> 00:00:03.660
has been talking about
it, and you've probably

00:00:03.660 --> 00:00:04.678
heard of it, too.

00:00:04.678 --> 00:00:06.720
It's become pretty popular,
and I think it's time

00:00:06.720 --> 00:00:07.885
to learn what it is.

00:00:07.885 --> 00:00:08.510
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:08.510 --> 00:00:10.218
With a whole universe
of things to learn,

00:00:10.218 --> 00:00:12.450
it's important to start
with the essentials.

00:00:12.450 --> 00:00:13.950
Let's go over the
concepts that make

00:00:13.950 --> 00:00:17.310
Kubernetes usable, scalable,
and just downright awesome.

00:00:17.310 --> 00:00:18.612
Are you ready?

00:00:18.612 --> 00:00:19.560
Hey, [INAUDIBLE].

00:00:19.560 --> 00:00:20.670
Good to see you.

00:00:20.670 --> 00:00:22.815
I was just going over
what Kubernetes is.

00:00:22.815 --> 00:00:24.600
SPEAKER 2: That sounds like fun!

00:00:24.600 --> 00:00:27.570
Kubernetes is a portable,
extensible, open-source

00:00:27.570 --> 00:00:29.820
platform for managing
containerized workloads

00:00:29.820 --> 00:00:33.480
and services that facilitates
both declarative configuration

00:00:33.480 --> 00:00:35.760
and automation, letting
you run distributed

00:00:35.760 --> 00:00:38.310
systems resiliently,
with scaling and failover

00:00:38.310 --> 00:00:39.555
for your application.

00:00:39.555 --> 00:00:42.300
SPEAKER 1: OK,
well, that's a lot.

00:00:42.300 --> 00:00:44.285
Do you have a way
to simplify that?

00:00:44.285 --> 00:00:45.780
SPEAKER 2: Good call.

00:00:45.780 --> 00:00:47.580
Basically, it's a
container orchestrator

00:00:47.580 --> 00:00:49.680
that helps make sure that
each container is where

00:00:49.680 --> 00:00:52.560
it's supposed to be and that the
containers can work together.

00:00:52.560 --> 00:00:54.480
SPEAKER 1: Oh,
that makes me think

00:00:54.480 --> 00:00:57.555
of a conductor that manages
everything in an orchestra.

00:00:57.555 --> 00:00:58.682
Are the horns going?

00:00:58.682 --> 00:01:00.390
Oh, the drums-- they
should be going now.

00:01:00.390 --> 00:01:01.980
SPEAKER 2: Exactly.

00:01:01.980 --> 00:01:05.220
There's a lot of moving points
in a scalable application.

00:01:05.220 --> 00:01:07.830
Just like a conductor
makes sure the song sounds

00:01:07.830 --> 00:01:10.290
like a composer
wants, Kubernetes

00:01:10.290 --> 00:01:12.090
makes sure services
are running smoothly

00:01:12.090 --> 00:01:13.740
the way an app developer wants.

00:01:13.740 --> 00:01:15.580
SPEAKER 1: Mmm.

00:01:15.580 --> 00:01:18.480
OK, before we get into more
details about the what,

00:01:18.480 --> 00:01:20.280
let's go over why
it was created.

00:01:20.280 --> 00:01:21.780
See, there are a
lot of applications

00:01:21.780 --> 00:01:23.350
that we call
monoliths, which means

00:01:23.350 --> 00:01:25.350
they put all the
functionality, like transaction

00:01:25.350 --> 00:01:28.080
and third-party integration,
into a single deployable

00:01:28.080 --> 00:01:28.620
artifact.

00:01:28.620 --> 00:01:30.780
SPEAKER 2: And monoliths
are a common way

00:01:30.780 --> 00:01:32.970
to build applications,
even today.

00:01:32.970 --> 00:01:34.950
But they still have
their downfalls.

00:01:34.950 --> 00:01:37.830
For example, deployments
can take a long time,

00:01:37.830 --> 00:01:40.357
since everything has to
roll out all together.

00:01:40.357 --> 00:01:41.940
And if different
parts of the monolith

00:01:41.940 --> 00:01:43.800
are managed by
different teams, there

00:01:43.800 --> 00:01:45.510
could be a lot of
additional complexity

00:01:45.510 --> 00:01:46.740
when prepping for a rollout.

00:01:46.740 --> 00:01:49.110
SPEAKER 1: And scaling
has the same problem.

00:01:49.110 --> 00:01:51.810
Teams have to throw resources
at the whole application,

00:01:51.810 --> 00:01:54.820
even if the bottleneck
is only on a single area.

00:01:54.820 --> 00:01:55.560
SPEAKER 2: Right.

00:01:55.560 --> 00:01:58.620
So people came up
with microservices.

00:01:58.620 --> 00:02:00.840
Each piece of functionality
is split apart

00:02:00.840 --> 00:02:03.390
into smaller
individual artifacts.

00:02:03.390 --> 00:02:05.880
If there's an update,
only that exact service

00:02:05.880 --> 00:02:07.127
has to be replaced.

00:02:07.127 --> 00:02:08.669
SPEAKER 1: And the
microservice model

00:02:08.669 --> 00:02:10.350
has scaling benefits, too.

00:02:10.350 --> 00:02:13.440
Now individual services can be
scaled to match their traffic,

00:02:13.440 --> 00:02:15.450
so it's easier to
avoid bottlenecks

00:02:15.450 --> 00:02:16.590
without over-provisioning.

00:02:16.590 --> 00:02:18.480
SPEAKER 2: This is
all great, but having

00:02:18.480 --> 00:02:20.670
one machine for
each service would

00:02:20.670 --> 00:02:24.330
require a lot of resources
and a whole bunch of machines.

00:02:24.330 --> 00:02:27.180
SPEAKER 1: That's why containers
are the perfect choice.

00:02:27.180 --> 00:02:29.700
With containers, teams can
package up their services

00:02:29.700 --> 00:02:30.430
neatly.

00:02:30.430 --> 00:02:32.550
All the applications,
their dependencies,

00:02:32.550 --> 00:02:35.905
and any necessary configuration
get delivered together.

00:02:35.905 --> 00:02:38.280
This also means that they can
be sure their services will

00:02:38.280 --> 00:02:40.990
run the same way, no
matter where they're run.

00:02:40.990 --> 00:02:43.470
SPEAKER 2: But there
are still more problems

00:02:43.470 --> 00:02:45.300
that remain unsolved.

00:02:45.300 --> 00:02:47.280
Upgrading a container
is easy, since you

00:02:47.280 --> 00:02:49.230
can create a new
version of the container

00:02:49.230 --> 00:02:51.360
and deploy it in
place of the old one.

00:02:51.360 --> 00:02:54.480
But how can upgrades be
done without downtime?

00:02:54.480 --> 00:02:57.630
How do these containers know
how to talk to the other ones?

00:02:57.630 --> 00:03:00.172
And how can the app
developer debug issues

00:03:00.172 --> 00:03:01.380
and observe what's happening?

00:03:01.380 --> 00:03:03.210
SPEAKER 1: And now
we've come back

00:03:03.210 --> 00:03:05.760
to the conductor of our
container orchestra.

00:03:05.760 --> 00:03:08.010
Kubernetes is all about
managing these containers

00:03:08.010 --> 00:03:10.380
on virtual machines or nodes.

00:03:10.380 --> 00:03:12.090
The nodes in the
containers they run

00:03:12.090 --> 00:03:14.040
are grouped together
as a cluster,

00:03:14.040 --> 00:03:16.710
and each container has
endpoints, DNS, storage,

00:03:16.710 --> 00:03:17.730
and scalability.

00:03:17.730 --> 00:03:19.830
SPEAKER 2: Everything
that modern applications

00:03:19.830 --> 00:03:22.970
need, without the manual
effort of doing it yourself.

00:03:22.970 --> 00:03:26.190
Kubernetes automates most of the
repetition and inefficiencies

00:03:26.190 --> 00:03:28.305
of doing everything by hand.

00:03:28.305 --> 00:03:29.940
The app developer
tells Kubernetes

00:03:29.940 --> 00:03:32.160
what it wants the
cluster to look like,

00:03:32.160 --> 00:03:33.630
and Kubernetes makes it happen.

00:03:33.630 --> 00:03:36.000
SPEAKER 1: See, this
all sounds amazing.

00:03:36.000 --> 00:03:38.775
So everyone should just
switch to Kubernetes, yeah?

00:03:38.775 --> 00:03:40.890
SPEAKER 2: Well, not so fast.

00:03:40.890 --> 00:03:44.730
Microservices still have
their own unique challenges,

00:03:44.730 --> 00:03:47.280
and sometimes a monolith
can be the right solution,

00:03:47.280 --> 00:03:50.010
based on what's right for
the application itself.

00:03:50.010 --> 00:03:53.070
And monoliths can still
run on Kubernetes,

00:03:53.070 --> 00:03:56.950
even though they won't be able
to use all the same benefits.

00:03:56.950 --> 00:03:58.770
SPEAKER 1: Either
way, how do you

00:03:58.770 --> 00:04:01.440
know when you're ready to
get started with Kubernetes?

00:04:01.440 --> 00:04:04.560
SPEAKER 2: Well, the first step
is to start using containers.

00:04:04.560 --> 00:04:06.360
That's easier said
than done, but let's

00:04:06.360 --> 00:04:08.280
save that for the next episode.

00:04:08.280 --> 00:04:10.710
SPEAKER 1: OK, so
now we have an idea

00:04:10.710 --> 00:04:13.470
of what kinds of problems
Kubernetes helps with,

00:04:13.470 --> 00:04:16.350
and we've also got an orbital
view of what it actually does.

00:04:16.350 --> 00:04:17.279
SPEAKER 2: Yep.

00:04:17.279 --> 00:04:19.624
Next time, let's
dig into containers.

00:04:19.624 --> 00:04:21.610
SPEAKER 1: Containers, huh?

00:04:21.610 --> 00:04:23.820
OK, stay tuned, everybody.

00:04:23.820 --> 00:04:25.533
If you want to get
hands on, check out

00:04:25.533 --> 00:04:26.700
the link in the description.

00:04:26.700 --> 00:04:29.430
And if you enjoyed the
episode, subscribe for more.

00:04:29.430 --> 00:04:32.480
[MUSIC PLAYING]

